1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,204
What happens when this heart valve cannot
open fully?

2
00:00:04,017 --> 00:00:09,276
The aortic valve is the doorway from the
heart’s main pumping chamber to the aorta.

3
00:00:09,973 --> 00:00:13,491
Watch how narrowing changes that opening
with each heartbeat.

4
00:00:14,002 --> 00:00:18,309
The left ventricle pumps blood through the
aortic valve into the aorta.

5
00:00:18,692 --> 00:00:21,861
This is the main artery carrying blood to
the body.

6
00:00:22,372 --> 00:00:25,402
The valve leaflets open as the chamber
pumps.

7
00:00:25,727 --> 00:00:30,208
They come together as the chamber relaxes,
helping keep blood moving in one

8
00:00:30,243 --> 00:00:31,277
direction.

9
00:00:31,973 --> 00:00:35,619
Aortic stenosis means the valve opening
has narrowed.

10
00:00:36,222 --> 00:00:41,319
In many older adults, calcium builds up on
the leaflets and makes them stiff.

11
00:00:42,016 --> 00:00:47,147
Some people are born with an aortic valve
that has two leaflets instead of the usual

12
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:51,896
three. That valve can also become narrowed
over time.

13
00:00:52,337 --> 00:00:56,458
The pumping chamber must generate more
pressure to move blood through a tighter

14
00:00:56,540 --> 00:01:01,311
opening. Over time its muscular wall can
thicken.

15
00:01:01,636 --> 00:01:06,582
The heart may have more trouble filling or
sending enough blood forward, especially

16
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:11,481
during activity. How much this affects a
person depends on the

17
00:01:11,528 --> 00:01:14,059
severity and how the heart adapts.

18
00:01:14,755 --> 00:01:17,159
Some people feel well for a long time.

19
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:22,964
Others notice breathlessness, chest
discomfort, dizziness, fainting or a drop

20
00:01:23,022 --> 00:01:24,960
in what they can do comfortably.

21
00:01:25,564 --> 00:01:29,419
A healthcare professional considers when
symptoms happen and what else could be

22
00:01:29,454 --> 00:01:33,830
causing them. A scan helps put those
symptoms in context.

23
00:01:34,341 --> 00:01:37,452
An echocardiogram is an ultrasound scan of
the heart.

24
00:01:37,836 --> 00:01:42,642
It can show how far the valve opens, how
fast blood moves through it, and how well

25
00:01:42,689 --> 00:01:47,449
the heart pumps. The team combines those
measurements with the person’s symptoms

26
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:52,824
and other findings. Sometimes more imaging
helps clarify the severity.

27
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:57,688
The next step may be regular monitoring or
assessment for valve replacement,

28
00:01:57,839 --> 00:02:01,137
depending on the severity and the whole
clinical picture.

29
00:02:01,740 --> 00:02:05,409
The team also considers how the condition
affects daily life.

30
00:02:05,851 --> 00:02:09,728
In the next film, we’ll see how
specialists use scans and a patient

31
00:02:09,763 --> 00:02:13,653
assessment to plan a possible catheter-
based valve replacement.
